    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Has a Taste of the e-MTB World with the RN0 Motor-Assisted Bicycle\nUnplugged Performance Wants To Continue Work on the Cybertruck, Should Tesla Give It the Green Light\n66 photos\nPhoto: Tesla\nUnplugged Performance, well-known for its Tesla performance parts, has offered to take the Cybertruck production line off Tesla's hands and continue to build the trucks while paying Tesla a license fee. If successful, this initiative will allow UP to expand the Cybertruck platform with an SUV and a cargo hauler. UP says it will fund production with a consortium of investors, allowing Tesla to focus on the Cybercab.\nWhen Elon Musk announced the demise of the Cybertruck, he said that Tesla needs factory space at Giga Texas to increase Cybercab production. The explanation did not convince many people, especially as Giga Texas has plenty of room for expansion. Besides that, Tesla had to admit that it currently can't even run the Cybercab line continuously, because it has no use for the vehicles it produces.\nIn a later tweet, Musk offered another explanation: that Tesla doesn't have enough talented people. Maintaining the Cybertruck while at the same time ramping up the Cybercab production would spread them too thin. This is more accurate, considering how many talented engineers have left Tesla over the past two years.\nEven though highly anticipated, considering the low sales numbers, the Cybertruck's demise signals a more disturbing trend: that Tesla is winding down its automotive operations, and the Cybertruck won't be the last to fall. As Tesla focuses on robots and robotaxis, perhaps its automotive business might be better off if offloaded to another company.\nThat's exactly what Unplugged Performance (UP) has proposed to Tesla, offering to buy the Cybertruck production line when it will be dismantled from Giga Texas next year. UP said that it's ready to line up a \"consortium of investors\" to run the line at no cost for Tesla.\nThis would be a win-win situation for everyone involved. Tesla gets to focus on Cybercab development and production, while freeing the company's resources, including floor space and headcount. Tesla also gains from license fees, plus FSD subscriptions and other revenue on every vehicle built.\nUnplugged Performance will also get important benefits. The upfitter has a wide range of Cybertruck parts and upgrades, while also upfitting the Cybertruck for use by police departments in the US. The Cybertruck's disappearance would leave it with a significantly diminished business.\nThe initiative has already gathered impressive support, not least from former Tesla engineers who reached out offering to help. Unplugged Performance admitted that the Cybertruck might end up more expensive , while peak output might be lower until the new owner gets up to speed. Unplugged Performance bets not only on the Cybertruck, but also on a three-row CyberSUV and a CyberCargo hauler to vastly expand production and make the Cybertruck platform profitable.\nA three-row SUV built on the Cybertruck's platform has been one of the most requested vehicles from Tesla fans. There's no reason to believe that it would not sell like hotcakes. Unplugged Performance also bets that vehicles built on the Cybertruck's platform could provide unique capabilities to Tesla's Robotaxi network, especially in situations where an off-road vehicle is required.\nDespite the excitement surrounding this initiative, I doubt that Tesla would agree to such a move. If, God forbid, Unplugged Performance proves the Cybertruck platform is successful and profitable, Elon Musk risks being exposed as an incompetent CEO . On the other hand, I'm sure Musk must have considered spinning off the automotive business, especially as Tesla's Chinese operations might be an obstacle to the upcoming SpaceX merger. A fair question we’re hearing: can UP really run the Cybertruck vehicle line? Not alone, and not overnight. Tesla expects to dismantle the line around mid-2027. Until then, we’re organizing interested investors, recruiting experienced manufacturing talent and partners, and choosing a location. We’d like to be near Giga Texas. Production would need to ramp back up, and it may be cell constrained. Peak output may be lower and unit costs may be higher. A reset is hard, premature death is harder. We choose reset. We believe this is highly fundable because one production platform serves three markets: existing demand for the pickup, pent-up demand for a seven-seat SUV, and Robotaxi network vehicles in useful all-terrain form factors (seven-seat people movers, cargo haulers, and pickups). Those markets have no feasible second best alternative with Tesla Autonomy. We’re full believers in Optimus. When Tesla has units ready, we aim to build a line where humans and humanoids work coordinated shifts around the clock. This depends on attracting people who believe in the mission: extending the joy of this cutting-edge technology platform onto roads not yet traveled. Tesla has set the bar themselves for what a qualified operator looks like, and we intend to meet it. There is perhaps no company as loyal or committed to Tesla as UP. We’ve built on Tesla platforms since 2013, and we’d be honored to carry the torch. We’re grateful for the outpouring of support from Tesla owners and shareholders, and we look forward to continuing the discussion. For those looking to contribute: [email protected] — UP (@UnpluggedTesla) November 11, 2026\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTesla Cybertruck Tesla Unplugged Performance up Elon Musk\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT Nobody Wants This Lamborghini Gallardo, Low-Mileage Supercar Fails To Sell",
